{
  "summary": "Updates the libbitcoinkernel C API and C++ wrapper to attach logging connections to contexts via btck_context_options_set_logger and configure minimum log levels per connection rather than globally. A logging connection receives no log messages until attached to a context, establishing the API interface required before per-context log separation is implemented.",
  "problem": "Every logging connection currently receives all kernel log output process-wide regardless of which context produced it, interleaving logs for applications managing multiple contexts (such as mainnet and signet or multi-context tests). Furthermore, the existing C API provides no way to attach a logging connection to a specific context, which would force breaking API changes and call-site refactors on downstream consumers once per-context routing is introduced.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'An application that opens two chainstates (say mainnet and signet, or a test creating several contexts) gets one interleaved log stream and can't tell which context a line came from.'",
    "PR description: 'Per-connection levels are a small feature, but the main purpose is to change the API now, before more code depends on it: #30342 builds on this to give each context's connection only that context's entries, so code like the above gets separate output per context unchanged.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#34374"
    ],
    "enables": [
      "#30342"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Only the final commit is unique to this PR; the preceding six commits are inherited from draft dependency #34374.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Rebased on top of #34374 to integrate with struct-based logging rather than string-based callbacks, and updated lifetime rules and abort semantics when connections are destroyed before contexts.",
  "card": "Modifies the libbitcoinkernel C API to attach logging connections to contexts via context options and configure log levels per connection. Currently, kernel logging connections receive an interleaved, process-wide stream across all contexts, with no mechanism to route context logs individually. This defines the API structure ahead of downstream adoption to enable per-context log isolation in follow-up work. Depends on #34374.",
